{"term":"mundana","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":40,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic literary term for a courtesan or prostitute, literally 'worldly woman,' evoking a woman immersed in secular, sensual life.","english_term":"woman of the world","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From Latin mundanus (worldly); used in 19th–early 20th century Brazilian fiction and journalism as a genteel circumlocution for sex worker; the sexual meaning is now largely opaque to younger speakers."}
{"term":"rameira","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":55,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic Portuguese term for a prostitute or sexually promiscuous woman, cognate with Spanish ramera.","english_term":"harlot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Folk etymology links it to ramo (branch), supposedly because women advertised brothels with branches at the door; used in classical Portuguese literature including Camões; largely unintelligible to modern Brazilians outside literary contexts."}
{"term":"fadista","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"demimonde","concept":"demimonde","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":22,"recognition_pct_peak":45,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Primarily a fado singer or devotee; historically carried a secondary connotation of a bohemian woman of loose morals living at the margins of respectable society.","english_term":"demimondaine","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The sexual implication derives from the disreputable associations of 19th-century Lisbon fado culture; in Brazil the term arrived through literature and carries almost no active sexual meaning today—recognition in a sexual sense is very low."}
{"term":"mulher da vida","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Euphemistic phrase meaning 'woman of the life/streets,' used to refer to a prostitute or sex worker without naming the profession directly.","english_term":"woman of the streets","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Still widely understood across generations in Brazil; declining in active use as sex worker discourse shifts to 'profissional do sexo'; the phrase frames the profession as an entire life trajectory rather than a single act."}
{"term":"mulher perdida","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"fallen_woman","concept":"promiscuous_woman_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'lost woman'; an archaic moral judgment applied to a woman who has had sex outside marriage or engaged in sex work, implying she is irreversibly ruined.","english_term":"fallen woman","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Rooted in Brazilian Catholic honor-shame culture; the 'loss' is of virtue, not location—once applied, the label was considered permanent and social rehabilitation nearly impossible; more about shame than commercial sex work specifically."}
{"term":"mulher de má vida","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sex_worker","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":32,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Archaic pejorative phrase meaning 'woman of bad life,' designating a prostitute or a woman of disreputable sexual morals.","english_term":"woman of ill repute","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Stronger moral condemnation than 'mulher da vida'; common in 19th–20th century Brazilian police reports, court documents, and Catholic moral discourse; differs from 'mulher perdida' by emphasizing ongoing conduct rather than a singular fall."}
{"term":"nudes","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"nude_images","concept":"nude_images","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":48,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Nude or sexually explicit photographs of oneself, typically shared or requested digitally; direct English loanword fully assimilated into Brazilian internet Portuguese.","english_term":"nudes","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Plural form used as a mass noun in pt-BR ('mandar nudes' = to send nudes); universal recognition across all demographics with internet access; appears in journalism, court cases, and sex education materials."}
{"term":"pack","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"nude_images","concept":"nude_images","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":55,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A bundled collection of intimate, nude, or explicit photos and videos of one person, typically sold, traded, or leaked in Brazilian internet communities.","english_term":"pack","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Culturally specific usage: in pt-BR internet culture, 'pack' implies a curated set of content attributable to a single person, often sold via Telegram or distributed non-consensually; distinct from 'nudes' (individual images) by its bundled, commodified nature."}
{"term":"vazar nudes","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"non_consensual_sharing","concept":"non_consensual_image_sharing","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":55,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":94,"recognition_pct_peak":94,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To leak someone's intimate or nude images without their consent, typically as revenge or for social humiliation.","english_term":"revenge porn","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Vazar' (to leak) applies broadly in Brazilian Portuguese; the phrase maps closely to the legal concept of 'exposição pornográfica não consensual' enshrined in Brazilian Law 13.718/2018; 'revenge porn' is the nearest English term but vazar nudes is neutral on motive."}
{"term":"sexting","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"sexting","concept":"sexting","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":35,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sending sexually explicit text messages, images, or videos via mobile device; English loanword used unchanged in Brazilian Portuguese.","english_term":"sexting","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Entered pt-BR circa 2010 with smartphone proliferation; used in journalism, sex education campaigns, and legal contexts; fully assimilated with no Portuguese phonological adaptation."}
{"term":"print íntimo","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"nude_images","concept":"nude_images","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":35,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A screenshot of an intimate, sexual, or personal conversation, photo, or video message, often shared without the subject's consent.","english_term":"intimate screenshot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Print' = screenshot (from 'print screen'); 'íntimo' = intimate; the phrase is itself neutral but appears most frequently in non-consensual sharing contexts; distinct from 'nudes' in being a screenshot rather than an original image."}
{"term":"conteúdo adulto","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"pornography","concept":"pornography","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":12,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Euphemistic label for sexually explicit material; the Brazilian Portuguese equivalent of 'adult content,' used by platforms, creators, and media to avoid explicit terminology.","english_term":"adult content","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Corporate and journalistic register; functions as a polite shield word covering everything from mild nudity to hardcore pornography; universally understood as a euphemism."}
{"term":"Only","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"onlyfans","concept":"onlyfans_platform","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":28,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Brazilian shorthand for OnlyFans; used as a noun for the platform or metonymically for online sexual content creation as a profession.","english_term":"OnlyFans","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"'Tem um Only' (has an Only) or 'faz Only' (does Only) signals that a person creates and sells explicit content online; the abbreviation is so common that 'Only' alone is understood in context without the full brand name."}
{"term":"cria conteúdo","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"sex_work_online","concept":"sex_work_online","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":12,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'creates content'; Brazilian internet euphemism for producing and selling explicit sexual material online, typically via subscription platforms.","english_term":"content creator (adult)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Functions as a socially legible euphemism understood by all internet users; the phrase is grammatically a third-person singular verb phrase used attributively ('ela cria conteúdo' = she does sex work online); plausible deniability in polite contexts."}
{"term":"live","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"camming","concept":"camming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Live streaming session; in sexual contexts, a real-time webcam performance for a paying or subscribing audience.","english_term":"live stream","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Live' in pt-BR can refer to any live stream (gaming, music, talk), not inherently sexual; the sexual meaning is context-dependent ('fazer uma live' on an adult platform); one of the most widely used English loanwords in Brazilian internet Portuguese."}
{"term":"webcam","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"camming","concept":"webcam_sex_work","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":22,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The device or the profession; in Brazilian internet slang, refers to the practice of performing sexually for pay via live video stream.","english_term":"webcam","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Dual meaning as both the physical device and the sex work practice; 'trabalhar de webcam' (to work via webcam) or 'modelo de webcam' are common formations; predates OnlyFans-era vocabulary and now coexists with newer terms."}
{"term":"cam","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"camming","concept":"camming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":25,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":83,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Shortened form of webcam; refers to webcam sex work or the performer engaged in it.","english_term":"cam","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More subculture-internal than 'webcam'; used in compounds ('camgirl,' 'camboy,' 'fazer cam'); largely understood by Brazilian internet users but less transparent to older or offline demographics."}
{"term":"sugar daddy","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"sugar_daddy","concept":"sugar_daddy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":91,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Older, typically wealthy man who provides financial support or gifts to a younger partner in exchange for companionship and sexual access; English loanword used unchanged.","english_term":"sugar daddy","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Widely recognized across Brazilian social media; pronounced with Portuguese phonetics by many speakers; associated specifically with apps such as SeekingArrangement (known in Brazil as 'aplicativo de sugar')."}
{"term":"sugar baby","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"sugar_dating","concept":"sugar_dating","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A younger person who receives money, gifts, or lifestyle support from a wealthier older partner in exchange for companionship and typically sexual access.","english_term":"sugar baby","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Female-coded in most Brazilian discourse but applies to any gender; heavily covered in Brazilian lifestyle journalism and true-crime content about exploitation within sugar relationships."}
{"term":"sugar","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"sugar_dating","concept":"sugar_dating","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Shorthand for the sugar dating lifestyle or arrangement, in which financial support is exchanged for companionship and sexual access.","english_term":"sugar dating","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Used both as a noun ('entrar no sugar') and modifier ('mundo sugar,' 'relacionamento sugar'); functions as an umbrella covering both the daddy and baby roles and the culture itself; more abstract than 'sugar daddy' or 'sugar baby.'"}
{"term":"affair","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"extramarital","concept":"love_affair","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":87,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An illicit or secret romantic and/or sexual relationship, especially extramarital; English loanword used unchanged in Brazilian Portuguese.","english_term":"affair","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used as a more sophisticated or cosmopolitan alternative to native 'caso'; implies mutual romantic entanglement rather than pure sexual opportunism; common in Brazilian social media discourse about infidelity."}
{"term":"amante","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"extramarital","concept":"extramarital_lover","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A lover, especially a secret or extramarital one; a person with whom one has a clandestine romantic or sexual relationship outside a primary partnership.","english_term":"lover / mistress","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Native Portuguese word, fully active in modern Brazilian usage; gender-neutral grammatically but socially marks both male and female lovers equally; placed in internet_subculture section due to high-volume infidelity discourse on social media, though the word itself predates the internet by centuries."}
{"term":"amigão","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"casual_sex","concept":"friends_with_benefits","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Augmentative of 'amigo' (friend); in internet slang, a male friend who is also a casual sexual partner, i.e., a friend with benefits.","english_term":"friend with benefits","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The augmentative -ão softens and slightly ironizes the implication of sexual involvement; the term implies ongoing casualness rather than a single encounter; the female equivalent in context is typically 'amiga' or 'amigona'; recognition in this specific sexual sense is lower than recognition of the word amigão in general."}
{"term":"caso","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"extramarital","concept":"love_affair","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":14,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A secret or illicit ongoing romantic and/or sexual relationship; the quintessential Brazilian Portuguese word for an affair, especially extramarital.","english_term":"affair","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'case' or 'situation'; 'ter um caso' (to have a case/affair) is the standard colloquial construction; implies duration and emotional investment beyond a one-night encounter; older and more native-feeling than 'affair' or 'fling'; universally understood by all Brazilian Portuguese speakers."}
{"term":"fling","lang":"pt-BR","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"internet_subculture","subcategory":"casual_sex","concept":"casual_sex_encounter","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A brief, casual romantic and/or sexual relationship without commitment; English loanword used in Brazilian Portuguese social media and dating app contexts.","english_term":"fling","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Less widespread than 'caso' or 'affair'; used mainly by younger Brazilians with strong English exposure; implies short duration and lightness rather than secrecy; coexists with native alternatives like 'ficada' (hookup) and 'rolo' (short fling)."}
